WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.620
Tor is a privacy network and a set of tools designed to reduce how much a single observer can learn about your browsing activity.

2
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:18.120
The name originally came from The Onion Router, a research project that explored how to route traffic through multiple layers instead of sending it straight from a person to a destination.

3
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:26.380
Today, people usually meet Tor through Tor Browser, which combines a modified browser with connection software that reaches the Tor network.

4
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:32.180
The basic problem Tor addresses is simple: ordinary web browsing creates many small records.

5
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.800
Your internet provider can see that you connected to a destination.

6
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.460
A public Wi-Fi operator may see where traffic is going if protections are weak.

7
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.400
A destination website can usually see the network address where the request appears to come from.

8
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:52.900
Advertising and analytics systems may connect visits across sites through identifiers, browser behavior, or account logins.

9
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.520
Tor does not erase every record, but it changes which parties can see which pieces.

10
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.920
Tor was created for legitimate privacy needs.

11
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.820
Journalists may need to research sensitive topics.

12
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.560
Activists may need to read blocked information.

13
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:13.100
Security researchers may need to examine public infrastructure without revealing their office network.

14
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.720
Everyday people may want a browser session that gives less information to a local network or an internet provider.

15
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:27.780
Those needs are not exotic; they are part of a healthy internet where people can read, learn, and communicate without being profiled by every network in the path.

16
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.100
The everyday version of this problem is easy to picture.

17
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.880
Imagine opening a browser on a hotel network, a school network, or a shared apartment connection.

18
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:42.980
Even when the page content is protected, the fact that a device contacted a certain service can be useful metadata.

19
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.920
Tor is designed to make that relationship harder to collect in one place.

20
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:53.500
It does not promise that nobody can observe anything; it changes the shape of observation so one party gets a narrower view.

21
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.340
A helpful way to understand Tor is to separate privacy from secrecy.

22
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:05.080
Privacy is about limiting unnecessary exposure and making observation harder to centralize.

23
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:10.460
Secrecy suggests that nothing can be seen, which is not how the internet works.

24
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:18.400
With Tor, a local observer can usually tell that Tor is being used unless a bridge or other access method changes that appearance.

25
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:27.860
A destination site may not see your original network address, but it can still see the content you submit, the account you log into, and the behavior you create on the site.

26
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.940
Tor also exists because trust on the internet is distributed unevenly.

27
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:39.600
You might trust a bank website to protect a login session, but not trust a coffee shop network to learn where you browse.

28
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.820
You might trust a community forum enough to read it, but not want that visit linked to your home connection.

29
00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:50.120
Tor tries to avoid putting complete trust in one network operator by spreading the path across relays.

30
00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:56.860
No single relay in a normal circuit is supposed to know both who you are and where you are going.

31
00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:01.860
The responsible lesson is not that Tor is a cloak or a shortcut around consequences.

32
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:06.280
It is a privacy tool with strengths, limits, and tradeoffs.

33
00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:14.220
It can help reduce exposure to some observers, especially local network observers and destination sites that would otherwise see your address.

34
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:21.680
It cannot make unsafe behavior safe, cannot protect information you voluntarily type into a site, and cannot replace judgment.

35
00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:27.540
This course treats Tor as practical privacy infrastructure, not as a myth.
